---
title: My Awesome Homepage
theme: dark
color: slate
headerStyle: clean
showStats: false
target: _blank
# Group order here IS display order, and a group services.yaml declares but this block omits
# renders below the laid-out ones at one column wide. A layout entry naming a group services.yaml
# does not declare is dead too, because layout entries match a group by name.
#
# The split is by WIDGET, not by topic. A widgeted tile is several lines tall and a link-only
# tile is one line, so mixing them in one group leaves ragged holes. Services and Calendar hold
# the widgeted tiles; Admin and Tools hold the link-only ones, four to a row.
layout:
  Top Row:
    style: row
    columns: 2
    header: false
    # THREE columns, and the number is derived from the tile count rather than chosen. Services
    # holds fifteen tiles, which divides by three exactly: five full rows, no orphan on the last
    # one and no hole beside it. See the calendar height in custom.css.j2, which is derived from
    # that row count so the two Top Row columns end level.
    #
    # Keep this in step with the tile count: a sixteenth tile both reintroduces the orphan and
    # leaves the calendar column short. Add three at a time, or re-derive the calendar height
    # alongside.
    Services:
      style: row
      columns: 3
    # FIVE columns for a group whose first tile is a calendar, because the personal links sit
    # under it. `columns` is uniform across a group, so the calendar would be a fifth of the
    # width — `#my-calendar { grid-column: 1 / -1 }` in custom.css.j2 spans it back across all
    # five, and the links then fill the row beneath.
    #
    # `style: row` rather than `column` is load-bearing: `column` renders the list as
    # `flex flex-col`, where grid-template-columns does nothing at all.
    Calendar:
      style: row
      columns: 5
    # DO NOT PUT `Bookmarks:` HERE. A BOOKMARK GROUP CANNOT LIVE INSIDE `Top Row` AT ALL, nested
    # under `Calendar:` or as a sibling of Services and Calendar, and homepage says nothing when
    # it refuses. The group falls out of Top Row and renders LAST at full width, which is exactly
    # what an unlaid-out group does. Nothing logs it and the repo checks stay green: only
    # reading the rendered page shows it. A bookmark group is only ever laid out as a TOP-LEVEL
    # entry.
    #
    # So the calendar column cannot hold the bookmarks. To put something under the calendar, it
    # has to be a SERVICE tile in services.yaml.j2's `Calendar` group, not a bookmark.
  # No `Media:` entry: that group does not exist, and a layout key naming no group is silently
  # dead. One link-only group: four tiles at four columns is one full row.
  Admin & Tools:
    style: row
    columns: 4
  # No `Bookmarks:` entry, and no bookmark group to lay out — the five personal links are
  # SERVICE tiles in the `Calendar` group, which is the only way to get them into that column.
  # See the note in `Top Row` above.
# Type-to-filter over every tile and bookmark on the page. This is what keeps the link-only
# Admin and Tools groups usable as they grow — the keyboard reaches a service whether or not
# its tile is in view. searchDescriptions widens the match to the `description:` line, so
# "download" finds qBittorrent through its "Download management" text.
quicklaunch:
  searchDescriptions: true
  hideInternetSearch: false
  hideVisitURL: false
providers:
  openweathermap: {{ weather_api_key }}
  # The longhorn INFO WIDGET reads its URL from here, not from its own entry in widgets.yaml.
  # Homepage splits that widget across the two files — `providers.longhorn` holds the connection,
  # widgets.yaml holds only the display options — and a `url:` written beside those options is
  # ignored, leaving the pod logging `<longhorn> Missing Longhorn URL` every refresh behind a
  # 1/1 Deployment.
  #
  # longhorn-frontend, not longhorn-backend, and the reasoning is in widgets.yaml.j2 beside the
  # display options.
  longhorn:
    url: http://longhorn-frontend.longhorn-system.svc.cluster.local
